---
const pains = [
  { title: 'Traffic', desc: 'A corridor begins slowing down before it becomes a citywide delay.' },
  { title: 'Waste', desc: 'A bin approaches capacity before it becomes an overflow and a complaint.' },
  { title: 'Energy', desc: 'Energy use rises abnormally before it becomes an avoidable cost.' },
];
const chain = ['Problem occurs', 'Someone notices', 'Report', 'Investigation', 'Decision', 'Response', 'Problem repeats'];
---
<section class="section problem" id="problem">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow">The real problem</span>
    <h2>Cities don't fail because they lack data. They fail when they discover problems too late.</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      The current model is usually reactive — wait for a visible failure, a complaint, a patrol, or a
      costly report, then investigate and respond.
    </p>

    <div class="pain-grid">
      {
        pains.map((p, i) => (
          <div class="pain-card">
            <span class="pain-num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <h3>{p.title}</h3>
            <p>{p.desc}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <div class="reactive-chain" role="group" aria-label="The reactive chain">
      {
        chain.map((step, i) => (
          <>
            <span class="chain-step">{step}</span>
            {i < chain.length - 1 && <span class="chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>}
          </>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <div class="problem-position">
      <p>
        NEBU is not a collection of sensors. It is not another dashboard. It is not an app asking
        citizens to report what the city should already know. It is the operating layer that connects
        the physical city, city teams, and citizens into one coordinated loop.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>